# Every path whose bytes `npm run render:check` hashes, pinned against end-of-line conversion.
#
# The font manifest records a SHA-256 for `public/fonts/fonts.css` and for each licence text, and
# the gate re-hashes what is on disk against it. Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its
# *system* config (`C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig`, measured with `git config --list
# --show-origin`), so a fresh checkout writes those text files with CRLF while the repository
# stores them with LF. On a clean worktree of 0a86e89 `fonts.css` came out 29,364 bytes with 724 CR
# bytes against this tree's 28,640 and none, and the gate reported 13 findings (six licence digest
# pairs plus the stylesheet) — while the same manifest and the same committed blobs were green.
#
# That makes the signed provenance record true only on a machine that happened to already hold the
# files, which is the opposite of what a digest is for. The three rules below describe the digested
# set exactly: what the gate hashes is what cannot be re-written at checkout.
#
# Scoped to `public/fonts/` on purpose. A repository-wide line-ending policy would re-normalise text
# committed with CRLF elsewhere, which is a different and far larger decision than this repair.
public/fonts/fonts.css text eol=lf
public/fonts/licences/*.txt text eol=lf
public/fonts/files/*.woff2 binary
